        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61821,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical presentation is usually pelvic or abdominal pain. Most cases are stage&nbsp;I, with a rare stage&nbsp;III case at presentation {26484981; 30575604}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61822,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>No epidemiological data are available on this very rare tumour. The majority of these tumours occur in postmenopausal women {26484981}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61823,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61824,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis remains unclear. Some tumours are thought to arise from mesonephric remnants in the paraovarian area; others may arise from M&uuml;llerian carcinomas that exhibit secondary mesonephric transdifferentiation. The latter hypothesis is supported by the association of mesonephric-like carcinomas with endometriosis, cystadenomas, adenofibromas, borderline tumours, and low-grade serous carcinomas {26484981; 30575604}. Of note, ovarian mesonephric-like carcinomas with coexisting serous borderline tumours or low-grade serous carcinomas show common molecular alterations (<em>KRAS</em> or <em>NRAS</em> mutations) in both components {30575604; 28863071}. <em>PIK3CA</em> mutations have been identified in some tumours {28984674}. <em>KRAS</em> mutations, loss of 1p, and gain of 1q are the most common molecular alterations; <em>NRAS</em> or <em>PIK3CA</em> mutations occur less frequently {28984674}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61825,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The tumours are usually unilateral, with variable size (4&ndash;32&nbsp;cm). The cut surface is solid or mixed solid and cystic, with a greyish-white or yellowish-tan appearance {26484981}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61826,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>The histological features, which are identical to those of mesonephric carcinomas occurring at other sites in the female genital tract, include tubular, glandular (pseudoendometrioid), ductal (with slit-like or angulated glands), papillary, and solid patterns. Eosinophilic colloid-like material can be present within the lumen. The nuclei show either dense or vesicular chromatin, inconspicuous nucleoli, and crowding. No squamous or mucinous elements are seen {26484981; 30148742}. No association with mesonephric remnants has been described. Associated endometriosis is common. By immunohistochemistry, most cases are positive for GATA3, TTF1, CD10 (luminal staining), and PAX8; are negative for ER, PR, and WT1; and show wildtype immunoreactivity for p53 {26484981; 30148742}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-02T17:53:25"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61827,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 382,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61828,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 382,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 472,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 385,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61829,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 385,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> histological features prototypical for mesonephric carcinoma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> positivity for GATA3 or TTF1; negativity for ER and PR.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 470,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 383,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61830,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 383,
        "headingText": "<p>Mesonephric-like carcinoma is staged according to the Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM classification (see <em>TNM staging of ovarian, fallopian tube, and primary peritoneal carcinoma</em>&nbsp;[[Brierley JD, Gospodarowicz MK, Wittekind C, editors. TNM classification of malignant tumours. 8th ed. Oxford (UK): Wiley-Blackwell; 2017.]]) and the FIGO staging system.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-15T11:09:38"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 471,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 384,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 61831,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 111,
        "headingId": 384,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Because of their rarity, the clinical behaviour of these tumours is unknown.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T16:57:32"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]